// End of lease and bond cleaning page, shipping at /house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning/.
//
// PROVENANCE, AND WHY IT MATTERS HERE. Every other page in this repo was
// built from a supplied content brief with approved copy. This one was NOT.
// There is no end-of-lease brief. The copy below is DRAFTED from
// "TLB Cleaning - Brand Foundation v2.0.md" (the BFD), which is a brand and voice
// document, not a content brief. So:
//
//   * Voice, terminology, CTA wording, the claim rules and the compliance
//     rules all trace to a real approved source (the BFD).
//   * The OPERATIONAL SUBSTANCE does not. The inclusions list, the
//     exclusions list, the re-clean policy, the timings and the pricing
//     answers are drafted from category convention, not from TLB's own
//     scope of works. Each one is flagged [CONFIRM] at its definition.
//
// Treat this page as a structured first draft for Teagan to correct, not as
// approved copy awaiting a proofread. content-plans/end-of-lease-cleaning.md
// lists every open item in one place.
//
// THE ONE RULE THIS PAGE MUST NOT BREAK. BFD 5.3 (Australian Consumer Law)
// and 3.5 (Guarantees) both say the same thing: do NOT promise a bond back.
//
//   "Never guarantee outcomes TLB cannot control (e.g. do not promise
//    '100% bond back guaranteed' unless the business has formally adopted
//    and can honour such a guarantee; otherwise use 'cleaned to inspection
//    standard' style language)."
//
// This is the single most tempting claim on the page and the category's
// competitors all make it. Nothing below promises a bond, a pass, or a
// refund. The page says "cleaned to the standard the inspection is measured
// against" and stops there. If Teagan formally adopts a guarantee, that is a
// deliberate decision to make once and then wire through this page, the FAQ
// and the meta description together. Do not add it piecemeal.
//
// TERMINOLOGY. BFD 3.3 says pick one consumer term and stay with it rather
// than alternating "bond clean" / "exit clean" / "vacate clean". BFD 2.3
// says use "bond clean" for consumers and "end of lease clean" for agency
// audiences. This page serves BOTH audiences, so it does the one thing that
// satisfies both rules: it states once, in section 2, that the names mean
// the same job, then uses "bond clean" in consumer-facing copy and "end of
// lease clean" in the agency-facing card and FAQ.
//
// Changed 15 Sep 2026. Section 2's synonym sentence now names "vacate
// clean", "exit clean" and "move out clean" as well, because all three are
// mapped secondaries that appeared nowhere in the copy and a Queensland-side
// lease usually says vacate or exit. They are named ONCE, in the sentence
// whose whole job is to settle the vocabulary, and are never used again.
// That is still BFD 3.3 being followed rather than broken: the rule is
// against alternating between them in running copy, not against telling a
// reader which words mean the same thing.
//
// RESERVED SLOTS. Photo and video slots with no `src` render as labelled
// dashed boxes at the right size. That is the correct state for a slot with
// no honest asset yet (IMAGE-GUIDELINES.md section 6). Never fill one with
// invented photos, quotes or names. Prompts for the outstanding slots are in
// src/assets/home_cleaning/inside_your_home/end_of_lease_and_bond_cleaning/IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
