// Section 0. Page metadata.
//
// The description deliberately does NOT say "bond back". It names the
// service, the area and what the page actually publishes, which is the
// strongest honest claim available. See the guarantee note in the file
// header.
//
// TITLE REVERTED TO THE WORKBOOK 16 Sep 2026, on TLB's instruction to ship
// the workbook copy verbatim. This is row 5's approved title, confirmed
// character-for-character against the workbook's own length column (46).
//
// What that instruction overrides, recorded so the trade is not lost:
//
//  1. The 15 Sep rewrite shipped "End of Lease Cleaning & Bond Cleans"
//     because it carries the exact-match primary `end of lease cleaning`
//     (4,800/mo, Keyword Mapping workbook 10 Aug 2026) contiguously and
//     picks up `bond cleaning services` (300/mo) with its second half.
//     "End of Lease Cleaning NSW & QLD" carries the primary contiguously
//     too, but drops the `bond cleaning` pickup for the jurisdictions.
//  2. It competes with the guide. /guides/what-the-law-actually-says-end-
//     of-lease-cleaning-in-nsw-and-qld/ ships as "End of Lease Cleaning
//     Requirements NSW & QLD", so a service page and a guide now sit in the
//     same SERP with near-identical titles. Watch which one Google picks;
//     if it prefers the guide for service intent, this is the first thing
//     to change back.
//
// 🚩 META SET FROM THE KEYWORD WORKBOOK 16 Sep 2026, on TLB's explicit
// instruction to ship the workbook copy verbatim. This reverses the 15 Sep
// ruling recorded here, which held that this line could NOT be published.
// The objections were not withdrawn on their merits, so they stand on the
// record and TLB has accepted them:
//
//  1. ⚠️ "Passes the first inspection" is an outcome claim. BFD 12.2
//     forbids guaranteeing an inspection result, and the guarantee note in
//     this file's header is the long version of why.
//  2. ⚠️ It contradicts this page's own FAQ 2, which says in as many words
//     that we cannot promise the agent signs off first time. A visitor who
//     clicks the SERP snippet lands on copy that takes the promise back.
//     These two cannot both be right; the body copy was left as-is because
//     the instruction was to change the meta, not the page.
//  3. ⚠️ "Fixed quote" contradicts §6 and §10, which both say condition
//     sets the price.
//
// Also note the geo: "across NSW and QLD" replaces the standing phrase
// "across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed", so this page now claims two
// whole states rather than the actual footprint. It reads wider than we are.
//
// 140 characters, so no truncation risk. If a tenant ever holds TLB to the
// first-inspection promise, this comment is the audit trail.
const pageMeta = {
  title: 'End of Lease Cleaning NSW & QLD | TLB Cleaning',
  description:
    'End of lease and bond cleaning that passes the first inspection, across NSW and QLD. We know both sets of rules. Get your fixed quote today.',
  // Domain still a placeholder, same as every other page. Confirm before launch.
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning/',
};

// PAGE-LOCAL CTA LABEL, added 15 Sep 2026 - the same override
// deep-cleaning.astro already carries, for the same reason and with the same
// scope.
//
// The shared `quoteCta` still reads "Get an instant quote" everywhere. On
// THIS page that contradicted the copy directly above and below it: BFD 13.2
// names bond cleans as work instant pricing works badly for, the hero lead
// says "fast quoting", and the pricing block argues at length that a bond
// clean cannot be priced sight unseen. The button was the last thing on the
// page still saying "instant".
//
// Overridden here rather than in navigation.ts because that export serves
// every page, including the genuinely instant-priceable ones. The site-wide
// split is still the Phase 2.3 / step 7 job in WEBSITE-UPDATE-PLAN-BFD-V2.md.
//
// The SITE HEADER's button keeps the shared label deliberately. It is a
// global component and changing it would relabel the regular-cleaning pages,
// where "instant" is correct per BFD 13.2. That needs a per-page label or a
// site-wide decision, not a copy edit on this file.
//
// `href` is unchanged, so it routes exactly where it did.
const pageQuoteCta = { ...quoteCta, label: 'Get a fast quote' };

const business = {
  name: 'TLB Cleaning',
  url: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/',
  logo: '',
  telephone: '+61 404 742 065',
  email: 'hello@tlbcleaning.com.au',
  address: {
    streetAddress: '',
    addressLocality: '',
    addressRegion: '',
    postalCode: '',
    addressCountry: 'AU',
  },
  sameAs: [],
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers NSW, the Tweed and Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  description: pageMeta.description,
};

// Section 1. Hero. Same shape as every other service page:
// split-single-image, image right, cut-out figure standing on the section
// edge via contain-bottom.
//
// The headline names the service AND the standard, which is the honest
// version of the promise a competitor makes with a guarantee badge.
//
// H1 gained the service term 15 Sep 2026. "Cleaned to the standard the
// inspection measures" carried none of the mapped primary; one word replaced
// puts `end of lease cleaning` at the front of the H1 verbatim and leaves the
// rhythm and the meaning of the original line intact.
//
// The lead's opening said "end of lease cleanS", a different word form, so
// the primary did not land in the opening paragraph either. It now does. The
// two-audience framing and the pricing line are untouched.
const hero = {
  headingLines: ['End of lease cleaning, to the standard the inspection measures'],
  lead: `End of lease cleaning for tenants who want their bond back, and for the agencies that manage the property. Both sides of the border, quoted before we start, with the full task list published on this page. ${theLine.fast}`,
  cta: pageQuoteCta,
  image: {
    // 1/1, like the mould page and for the same reason: the panel is a
    // `contain` fit, so a canvas cropped to the subject lets the figure scale
    // to the full panel instead of being shrunk by empty transparent margin.
    // The canvas keeps ~12% transparent headroom above the raised squeegee
    // (1080x1080 around a 656x950 subject) so the tool has air over it rather
    // than butting into the top of the section.
    ratio: '1/1',
    label:
      'TLB Cleaning cleaner in an aqua polo, teal apron and teal gloves holding a window squeegee and a microfibre cloth',
    src: heroImg,
  },
};

// Section 2. Definition paragraph. The answer-target block for
// `bond cleaning` and `end of lease cleaning`, and the GEO passage most
// likely to be lifted whole by an AI overview (BFD 3.6.2 asks for
// definition-style sentences and semantic triples, so the first sentence is
// a definition and the second is subject-predicate-object).
//
// The second paragraph is where the page settles its terminology once, so
// the rest of the page can use both names without reading as two services.
const definitionBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'A bond clean is a comprehensive end of lease clean, done to the standard your exit inspection is measured against rather than to the standard of an everyday tidy. TLB Cleaning provides bond and end of lease cleaning across the Northern Rivers, the Tweed and the Southern Gold Coast.',
  // Widened 15 Sep 2026. `move out house cleaning` (80/mo), `vacate
  // cleaning` and `exit clean` (both unmeasured rather than absent, Keyword
  // Mapping workbook 10 Aug 2026) are mapped secondaries that appeared
  // nowhere on the page. This is the one sentence on the site whose job is to
  // settle the vocabulary, so it is the one place they can be named without
  // the page reading as five services. See the TERMINOLOGY note in the file
  // header before shortening this back.
  'Bond clean, end of lease clean, vacate clean, exit clean and move out clean are all names for the same job. Tenants tend to say bond clean because the bond is what is at stake, agencies tend to say end of lease clean because the tenancy is what is ending, and Queensland-side leases often say vacate or exit. We use all of them on this page and mean the same thing by them.',
];

// Section 3. Pathway cards. The page has two genuinely different readers
// (BFD 4.1 audiences 1 and 3) and they want opposite things from it: a
// tenant wants to know what it costs and whether it will pass, a property
// manager wants capacity and turnaround. Rather than write one blended page
// that serves neither, the cards split them at the top.
//
// Card 3 lands on section 8's comparison table, the same #compare pattern
// deep-cleaning.astro uses.
//
// Section heading/accent and the three CTA labels are DRAFTED, not briefed.
const pathwayCards = {
  heading: 'Two people read this page.',
  headingAccent: 'Which one are you?',
  cards: [
    {
      number: 1,
      image: { ratio: '4/3', label: 'Empty room on moving-out day', src: cardTenantImg },
      title: "I'm moving out and I want my bond back",
      description:
        'Then you want the clean measured against the entry condition report, not against how clean the place feels. That is what this page is about.',
      cta: pageQuoteCta,
    },
    {
      number: 2,
      image: {
        ratio: '4/3',
        label: 'Property manager with a tablet and keys at the bench of an empty rental',
        src: cardAgencyImg,
      },
      title: 'I manage the property',
      description:
        'We already hold ongoing contracts with major agencies across the region. Vacancy windows are tight, so tell us the handover date and we will work back from it.',
      cta: { label: 'Real estate and property management', href: '/house-cleaning/real-estate-cleaning/' },
    },
    {
      number: 3,
      image: {
        ratio: '4/3',
        label: 'Homeowner comparing cleaning options on a tablet',
        src: cardCompareImg,
      },
      title: "I'm not sure this is the clean I need",
      description:
        'Fair enough. A bond clean is not the same job as a deep clean, and it is priced differently. Here is how the three compare.',
      cta: { label: 'Compare the three', href: '#compare' },
    },
  ],
};

// Section 4. What's included in a bond clean.
//
// [CONFIRM THE WHOLE LIST WITH TEAGAN.] This is the most consequential block
// on the page and the one with the weakest provenance. It is drafted from
// what a bond clean conventionally covers in NSW and QLD, NOT from TLB's own
// scope of works, because no scope of works was supplied.
//
// It matters more than the equivalent list on the deep-cleaning page,
// because a tenant will hold TLB to it against money. If TLB's real scope
// differs on even one line (ovens, tracks, walls, garages), this page has
// promised something the team is not pricing for. Whoever quotes these jobs
// has to read it line by line before launch.
//
// SHAPE. Same as deep-cleaning section 5: ContentGrid `text` cells, not
// ServiceBlocks `icon-grid`, because ServiceIcon.astro has no room icons and
// six rooms would share three unrelated glyphs. Tasks run as one
// comma-separated sentence per room rather than bullets, matching the
// deep-cleaning page so the two lists read as one company's standard.
//
// Six cells in two columns is three clean rows, so nothing needs a span here
// (unlike deep-cleaning's orphaned fifth cell).
const inclusionBlocks = [
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'Kitchen',
    body: [
      'Inside and outside of all cupboards and drawers, benchtops, splashback, sink and taps, rangehood and filter, oven inside and out including racks and trays, cooktop, the fridge cavity, dishwasher filter and seals, floors including edges and corners.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'Bathrooms, ensuites and toilets',
    body: [
      'Shower screen, tiles and grout, shower base and drain, bath, vanity inside and out, basin, taps and fittings, mirrors, exhaust fan cover, towel rails, toilet inside and out including the base and the cistern, floors and skirting.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'Bedrooms and living areas',
    body: [
      'Inside wardrobes, shelves and drawers, skirting boards, doors and door frames, architraves, light switches and power points, window sills and tracks, inside windows, ceiling fans, cobwebs, air vents, full vacuum including edges and under moveable furniture, mop.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'Laundry',
    body: [
      'Tub inside and out, benches, cupboards inside and out, behind and beside appliances where they can be safely moved, dryer lint filter, taps and fittings, floors and skirting.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'Walls, marks and spots',
    body: [
      'Spot cleaning of scuffs and marks on walls and doors where the paint surface allows it. Some marks are damage rather than dirt and will not come out with cleaning. We tell you which is which rather than scrubbing through the paint and turning a mark into a repair.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'Throughout',
    body: [
      'Light fittings and shades, air vents and returns, switchplates, door handles, blind and curtain dusting, all floors including edges, corners and behind doors.',
    ],
  },
];

// Section 4b. The two exclusion groups. Callout, not two more grid cells:
// they are the opposite of everything above them, and an inset panel that
// reads as an interruption is exactly Callout's job.
//
// [CONFIRM], and this is the higher-stakes half. On a bond clean an
// unexpected exclusion is not just a complaint, it is a line item on an exit
// report and a deduction from someone's bond. The FAQ "What isn't included
// in a bond clean?" restates this list, so the two have to be settled
// together and kept in step.
//
// The carpet line is the one to check first. In both NSW and QLD a lease may
// specifically require professional carpet cleaning, so a tenant reading
// "quoted separately" needs to understand it is available and commonly
// needed, not that TLB will not do it.
const exclusionsCallout = {
  body: [
    'Quoted separately: carpet steam cleaning, exterior windows, pest control, garages, balconies and outdoor areas, walls needing a full wash rather than spot cleaning, and any cleaning at height. Many leases specifically require professional carpet cleaning at the end of a tenancy, so ask for it with your quote rather than after the inspection.',
    'Not included: repairs, painting, gardening or lawns, rubbish and hard waste removal, or anything that needs a licensed trade. If the property needs one of those, we say so when we quote rather than leaving you to find out at the inspection.',
  ],
};

// Section 5. How the standard is set. This is the section that does the work
// a bond-back guarantee would have done, honestly.
//
// The argument: a bond clean is not measured against "clean", it is measured
// against the entry condition report, allowing for fair wear and tear. That
// is genuinely useful to a tenant who has never done this before, and it is
// the reason TLB can be confident about the clean without promising an
// outcome that depends on the agent, the report and the state of the
// property before anyone arrived.
//
// LEGAL REVIEW. These three cells describe tenancy process in NSW and QLD.
// They are written generally and deliberately avoid stating any timeframe,
// dollar figure or entitlement, because those differ by state and change.
// Even so, this is the one block on the site that describes how a legal
// process works. It should be read by someone who can confirm it before
// launch, and outbound links to NSW Fair Trading and the Queensland
// Residential Tenancies Authority should be added (BFD 3.3 asks for
// authoritative external sources) once there is a guides section to host
// them.
const standardBlocks = [
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'It starts with the entry report',
    // Middle sentence added 15 Sep 2026. "Reasonably clean" is the statutory
    // phrase every tenancy resource on this SERP uses, and a searcher will
    // usually have read it somewhere else before landing here. It was absent
    // from this page entirely.
    //
    // Deliberately stated at the level of what the law asks for, with no Act,
    // section, timeframe or entitlement named. The detail on the two Acts,
    // the two regulators and the two tribunals stays on
    // /guides/what-the-law-actually-says-end-of-lease-cleaning-in-nsw-and-qld/,
    // which is where it can be dated and reviewed. Do not "improve" this by
    // adding a specific without a compliance read - see the LEGAL REVIEW note
    // above.
    body: [
      'Your exit inspection is compared against the condition report from the day you moved in, not against a blank standard. The law in both states asks for the property to be left reasonably clean and in the condition it started in, fair wear and tear aside. So if a mark was there on day one and the report says so, it is not something the clean has to fix.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'Fair wear and tear is not dirt',
    body: [
      'Carpet that has aged, paint that has faded and fittings that have worn are not cleaning failures. We clean what cleaning can change, and we tell you plainly when something is damage or age instead.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'The agent still holds the pen',
    body: [
      'We clean to the standard the inspection measures. We are not in the room when it happens, and no cleaner can promise you what an agent will write in a report, so we would rather be straight with you about that than sell you a guarantee.',
    ],
  },
];

// Section 6. If the agent comes back. THE section a competitor would replace
// with a bond-back guarantee badge.
//
// [CONFIRM THE RE-CLEAN POLICY WITH TEAGAN BEFORE LAUNCH. This is the
// highest-risk unconfirmed thing on the page.] The copy below commits TLB to
// returning, within a stated window, at no charge, for items on the report
// that were in the agreed scope. That is a promise about money and time,
// drafted here from category convention because a page that says nothing
// about what happens next is worse than useless to a nervous tenant.
//
// Three things still need Teagan's answer:
//   1. The window (competitors typically offer 72 hours to 7 days).
//   2. Whether the re-clean is free for in-scope items only, which is what
//      is written, or broader.
//   3. Whether the client has to notify TLB before responding to the agent.
//
// If Teagan does not want to offer a re-clean at all, DELETE this section
// and its matching FAQ rather than softening them. A vague promise here is
// worse than no promise.
//
// BRACKET REMOVED FROM THE SECOND PARAGRAPH, 15 Sep 2026. It read
// "[CONFIRM: the re-clean window, for example 72 hours or 7 days]" and it
// was rendering to READERS, in the middle of the paragraph that carries
// TLB's whole answer to two competitors' bond-back guarantees. The
// replacement below is publishable today and invents no window.
//
// SWAP IT BACK THE MOMENT THE WINDOW IS CONFIRMED. A stated number is
// stronger than general urgency, and the sentence to restore is:
//   'Let us know within 72 hours of the inspection, so we can get back in
//    before the keys are gone and the matter moves on without us.'
// with 72 hours replaced by whatever Teagan actually confirms. The matching
// FAQ answer ("Let us know quickly, before the keys change hands") moves
// with it.
const reCleanBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'If the agent lists something from our task list on the exit report, tell us and we will come back and put it right at no charge. That covers the items we quoted for. It does not cover things that were never in scope, like a carpet steam clean nobody booked, or damage that cleaning was never going to fix.',
  'Tell us as soon as the report lands, because the window to fix something closes when the keys change hands. Ring us the same day if you can.',
  'This is a re-clean commitment, not a bond guarantee. What an agent writes in a report is their call and not ours, so we promise the part we actually control, which is the work.',
];

// Section 6b. What we give you for the agent. NEW, 15 Sep 2026.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. Two reasons, and neither is padding.
//
// Search: it is the only home on the page for `end of lease cleaning company`
// (200/mo, Keyword Mapping workbook 10 Aug 2026), which had no coverage at
// all. The phrase is what somebody types when they want a business with
// paperwork behind it rather than a person with a bucket.
//
// Competitors: the 15 September audit found Jim's Cleaning leads both its NSW
// and its Byron Bay end of lease pages on full receipts and service dockets
// for the property manager. This page's FAQ 6 already tells a tenant the
// agent will want a receipt, and then never says TLB provides one. That is
// the gap this closes.
//
// It also does real work for section 6 above: the invoice is the document
// that settles what was in scope, which is how a free re-clean happens
// without an argument.
//
// CONFIRM BEFORE LAUNCH, and this is an operational claim about a document a
// client will hold TLB to:
//   1. That invoices are itemised to TASK level, not just to "bond clean".
//   2. That carpet steam cleaning is separately receipted when it is booked.
// If either is not true today it is a small change to how invoices are
// issued, not a reason to soften the copy - but the copy cannot ship ahead of
// the change.
const agentPaperworkBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Agents rarely take your word for it, so we give you the paper. You get an itemised invoice showing the property, the date and the work done, and it lists the same tasks published on this page. If the agent queries a line, you can hold one against the other.',
  'If carpet steam cleaning was part of the booking, it comes as its own line with its own receipt. A carpet clause in a lease is usually satisfied by a receipt rather than by an inspection.',
  'And if something on our list is picked up on the exit report, the invoice is what tells us what we quoted for. That is how the free re-clean gets sorted without an argument about scope.',
];

// Section 7. Both sides of the border. A genuine local differentiator, and
// the reason this page names two states where other pages quietly drop the
// Queensland half.
//
// TLB's service area straddles the NSW/QLD border at Tweed Heads and
// Coolangatta. A tenant moving from Tweed Heads to Coolangatta changes legal
// jurisdiction while moving four kilometres, and most cleaners on either
// side work only one of them. This is exactly the "reinforce the local
// advantage" theme in BFD 4.5.
//
// The copy names the two regulators as the bodies that set the rules, which
// is factual and is the kind of authoritative reference BFD 3.3 asks for. It
// makes no claim about what either body requires. Keep it that way unless
// the claim is checked.
const borderBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Our service area crosses the border, so we clean to both sets of rules as a matter of routine. A tenancy in Ballina, Byron or Lismore sits under NSW Fair Trading. A tenancy in Coolangatta, Palm Beach or Burleigh Heads sits under the Queensland Residential Tenancies Authority.',
  'The cleaning itself does not change much between the two. What changes is the paperwork around it, and which condition report your inspection is measured against. If you are moving from one side of the border to the other, which plenty of people here do, you can book the clean at both ends with the same team.',
];

// Section 7b. Before you book, three things worth reading. NEW, 15 Sep 2026.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. All three end of lease guides link TO this page and this
// page linked to none of them, so the internal linking ran one way only and
// the page passed nothing back to the three articles that support it.
//
// ANCHOR TEXT IS WRITTEN HERE rather than taken from guides.ts' shared
// `shortLabel` and `blurb`, which is the exception to this codebase's usual
// rule. Two reasons, both deliberate. The shared labels are shortened for
// pill lists ("The end of lease checklist", "What a bond clean costs") and
// dropping "cleaning" is exactly the word the link is supposed to pass. And
// each guide holds its own mapped target - `end of lease cleaning
// requirements` 150/mo, `end of lease cleaning checklist` unmeasured,
// `end of lease cleaning cost` 800/mo (workbook, 10 Aug 2026, rows 61-63) -
// so the anchors are written to hand those phrases over rather than to
// compete with them from a service page.
//
// The `href` and the icon still come from guides.ts, so a slug change stays
// a one-file fix and the GuideHref union still makes a link to a guide that
// does not exist a type error rather than a 404 a reader finds.
//
// The lead's last clause ("one of them tells you when you do not need to
// hire anybody") is the BFD 9.1 "straight with people" rule doing the same
// job pathway card 3 does: routing a reader away from the sale when the sale
// is not what they need.
const readingGuide = (href: GuideHref, title: string, description: string) => ({
  icon: guidePages.find((guide) => guide.href === href)!.icon,
  title,
  description,
  href,
});

const readingGuides = [
  readingGuide(
    '/guides/what-the-law-actually-says-end-of-lease-cleaning-in-nsw-and-qld/',
    'What the law actually says',
    'The condition report standard in NSW and Queensland, what fair wear and tear covers, and when a professional clean can and cannot be insisted on.'
  ),
  readingGuide(
    '/guides/the-end-of-lease-cleaning-checklist/',
    'The end of lease cleaning checklist',
    'Room by room, in the order the job is actually worked, with the lines agents mark first.'
  ),
  readingGuide(
    '/guides/how-much-does-end-of-lease-cleaning-cost/',
    'What an end of lease clean costs',
