// page's second CTA, and it argues rather than lists.
//
// [CONFIRM] on the second paragraph. "You get a price up front" is a process
// promise. It matches the instant-quote positioning in BFD 1.5
// differentiator 5, but confirm the quoting flow actually returns a price
// for bond cleans rather than a callback, because bond cleans are
// condition-dependent in a way regular cleans are not.
//
// First paragraph added 15 Sep 2026. The section opened procedurally, on
// "Book online in a couple of minutes", which is an instruction to somebody
// who has already decided. The new opener agitates something the reader is
// actually feeling at this point in a move (BFD 5.2) and does it without
// implying their house or their organisation is the problem. The three
// paragraphs under it are good and are untouched.
const bookingBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Most bond cleans get booked in the worst week of the move, when the truck is late, the keys are due and nobody has eaten a proper meal in three days. Book it the day the handover date lands instead, and it becomes one fewer thing between you and the end of it.',
  'Book online in a couple of minutes, or send a text, ring us or drop us an email, whichever you would actually do. Tell us the handover date when you book, because that is the date everything works back from.',
  'You get a price up front rather than waiting on a callback. It is based on the property as it actually is, so be honest about the condition when you book. Nothing you tell us will surprise us, and it means we allow the right amount of time instead of running short on the day.',
  'Book the clean for after the removalists and before the final inspection, with a day in between if you can manage it. An empty property cleans faster and better, and that gap is what gives everyone room if something needs a second look.',
];

// Section 12. Social proof - HOLDING COPY, 15 Sep 2026.
